// AAMC Question Bank Flashcards — Independent Question Bank (Psychology, Sociology, Biochemistry): original recall
// questions written from the concepts tested. No AAMC questions or explanations are reproduced.
// Format: {s: tag, disc: scoring discipline, aamc: content category, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, src}
const QB_AAMC_IQB = [
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'A cyclist falls and lies injured on a crowded plaza. Compared with an identical fall witnessed by a single passerby, help on the crowded plaza is most likely to be:',choices:['faster, because combined vigilance is greater','slower, because responsibility is diffused','faster, because others model helping','unchanged, because helping reflects traits'],ans:1,exp:'In the **bystander effect**, each added onlooker lowers the chance and speed that any one person helps, largely through **diffusion of responsibility** (each assumes someone else will act). Larger crowds do not add up to faster help, even though more people could notice the emergency.',src:'aamc-iqb:P1'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7B',q:'Haze drifts from a vent during an exam, but each student stays seated because no one else appears worried, and each concludes the situation is not an emergency. This best illustrates:',choices:['Social loafing','Deindividuation','Group polarization','Pluralistic ignorance'],ans:3,exp:'**Pluralistic ignorance** occurs when individuals privately doubt a situation but read others\' calm behavior as evidence that nothing is wrong, so no one acts. It is a key contributor to the bystander effect, alongside diffusion of responsibility.',src:'aamc-iqb:P1b'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8B',q:'A student credits her high physics score to her own talent but blames her low organic chemistry score on a poorly written exam. This pattern is called:',choices:['Self-serving bias','Fundamental attribution error','Actor–observer bias','Just-world hypothesis'],ans:0,exp:'The **self-serving bias** attributes successes to internal, personal factors and failures to external, situational ones, protecting self-esteem. The fundamental attribution error concerns explaining OTHER people\'s behavior, overemphasizing their dispositions.',src:'aamc-iqb:P2'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8B',q:'When a new coworker misses a deadline, Priya decides he is careless, overlooking that his computer crashed that week. Priya is displaying:',choices:['Self-serving bias','False consensus effect','Fundamental attribution error','Optimism bias'],ans:2,exp:'The **fundamental attribution error** is the tendency to overestimate dispositional causes and underestimate situational causes when explaining others\' behavior. It is especially strong in individualistic cultures; self-serving bias concerns one\'s own outcomes.',src:'aamc-iqb:P2b'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8B',q:'After a driver swerves into another lane, she explains that a dog ran into the road, while a witness concludes she is reckless. This discrepancy best reflects:',choices:['Halo effect','Self-serving bias','Confirmation bias','Actor–observer bias'],ans:3,exp:'In **actor–observer bias**, actors attribute their own behavior to the situation, while observers attribute the same behavior to the actor\'s dispositions. Actors can see the situational pressures on them, whereas observers mostly see the person.',src:'aamc-iqb:P3'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8B',q:'According to Kelley\'s covariation model, a behavior is most likely attributed to the situation rather than the person when it shows:',choices:['high consensus, high distinctiveness and high consistency','low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency','low consensus, high distinctiveness and low consistency','high consensus, low distinctiveness and low consistency'],ans:0,exp:'If most people behave the same way (**high consensus**), the person acts this way only toward this stimulus (**high distinctiveness**) and does so reliably (**high consistency**), the cause is attributed externally. Low consensus and low distinctiveness with high consistency point to an internal, dispositional cause.',src:'aamc-iqb:P3b'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'A sunscreen ad persuades viewers mainly by showing a famous, attractive athlete rather than presenting data on skin-cancer risk. In the elaboration likelihood model, this ad relies on:',choices:['the central route','the peripheral route','cognitive dissonance','the foot-in-the-door technique'],ans:1,exp:'The **peripheral route** persuades through superficial cues such as source attractiveness, fame or prestige rather than argument quality. The **central route** depends on careful evaluation of strong arguments and evidence, and it produces more durable attitude change.',src:'aamc-iqb:P4'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'According to the elaboration likelihood model, a listener is most likely to process a health message through the central route when the listener is:',choices:['distracted and pressed for time','uninterested in the topic','focused on the speaker\'s appearance','motivated and able to weigh arguments'],ans:3,exp:'Central-route processing requires both **motivation** (e.g., personal relevance) and **ability** (knowledge, time, few distractions). When either is low, people fall back on peripheral cues such as the speaker\'s attractiveness or credentials.',src:'aamc-iqb:P4b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'Which reinforcement schedule produces a high, steady response rate with no predictable post-reinforcement pause and the greatest resistance to extinction?',choices:['Fixed-ratio','Variable-interval','Variable-ratio','Fixed-interval'],ans:2,exp:'On a **variable-ratio** schedule, reinforcement follows an unpredictable number of responses (as with slot machines), so responding stays fast and persists long after reinforcement stops. Fixed-ratio schedules also give high rates but show a pause after each reinforcer.',src:'aamc-iqb:P5'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'A factory worker is paid after every 30 parts assembled and typically takes a short break right after each payment. This pattern is characteristic of a:',choices:['variable-ratio schedule','fixed-ratio schedule','fixed-interval schedule','variable-interval schedule'],ans:1,exp:'A **fixed-ratio** schedule reinforces after a set number of responses, producing high response rates with a brief **post-reinforcement pause**. Fixed-interval schedules instead produce a scalloped pattern, with responding accelerating as the reinforcement time approaches.',src:'aamc-iqb:P5b'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7A',q:'In Freud\'s structural model, which component mediates between the impulses of the id and the moral demands of the superego, using defense mechanisms to reduce the resulting anxiety?',choices:['Id','Superego','Ego ideal','Ego'],ans:3,exp:'The **ego** operates on the reality principle, balancing id drives against superego standards; unresolved conflict creates aversive tension that the ego reduces with defense mechanisms. The ego ideal is part of the superego, representing the standards one strives toward.',src:'aamc-iqb:P6'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6C',q:'Drive-reduction theory proposes that behavior is motivated mainly by the need to:',choices:['reduce aversive internal tension','maintain an optimal level of arousal','achieve self-actualization','obtain external rewards'],ans:0,exp:'**Drive-reduction theory** (Hull) holds that physiological needs create aversive drives, and behavior aims to reduce them and restore homeostasis. Maintaining an optimal level of arousal describes arousal theory, which better explains thrill-seeking that increases tension.',src:'aamc-iqb:P6b'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7A',q:'Identical twins share a genetic vulnerability to major depression, but only the twin who loses a job and a parent in the same year develops the disorder. This is best explained by:',choices:['the social drift hypothesis','the monoamine hypothesis','the diathesis–stress model','learned helplessness theory'],ans:2,exp:'The **diathesis–stress model** states that a predisposition (diathesis, often genetic) produces a disorder only when combined with sufficient environmental stress. Because the twins share the same genes, the differing outcome points to the stressor as the trigger.',src:'aamc-iqb:P7'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7A',q:'The concordance rate for schizophrenia in identical (monozygotic) twins is roughly:',choices:['about 10%','about 50%','about 1%','about 100%'],ans:1,exp:'Monozygotic twin concordance for schizophrenia is about **40–50%**, compared with roughly 10–15% for dizygotic twins and about 1% in the general population. Because it is well below 100%, genes alone are not sufficient, which supports the diathesis–stress model.',src:'aamc-iqb:P7b'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7B',q:'Jurors who initially lean slightly toward a long sentence recommend an even longer sentence after deliberating together. This shift is called:',choices:['Group polarization','Groupthink','Social loafing','Deindividuation'],ans:0,exp:'In **group polarization**, discussion strengthens a group\'s initial average leaning, pushing members toward a more extreme version of it. Groupthink is a different failure, in which the desire for harmony suppresses dissent and realistic evaluation of alternatives.',src:'aamc-iqb:P8'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7B',q:'A cohesive hospital board with a directive chair, eager to reach consensus, dismisses safety warnings and discourages dissent before approving a risky expansion. This best illustrates:',choices:['Group polarization','Social facilitation','Deindividuation','Groupthink'],ans:3,exp:'**Groupthink** arises in cohesive, insulated groups with directive leaders, where the drive for unanimity produces self-censorship, an illusion of consensus and poor decisions. Group polarization describes attitudes becoming more extreme after discussion, not suppression of dissent.',src:'aamc-iqb:P8b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'Animals exposed to unavoidable shocks later make no attempt to escape shocks that they could easily avoid. This phenomenon is:',choices:['Extinction','Latent learning','Learned helplessness','Habituation'],ans:2,exp:'**Learned helplessness** (Seligman) develops when repeated uncontrollable aversive events teach an organism that its actions do not matter, producing passivity even after escape becomes possible. It is a classic animal model of depression.',src:'aamc-iqb:P9'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7A',q:'The reformulated learned-helplessness model links depression to explaining negative events with causes that are:',choices:['external, unstable and specific','internal, unstable and specific','internal, stable and global','external, stable and global'],ans:2,exp:'A pessimistic **attributional style** explains bad events as internal ("it is my fault"), stable ("it will always be this way") and global ("it ruins everything"), and it predicts depression. External, unstable, specific attributions are protective.',src:'aamc-iqb:P9b'},
  {s:'Sensation, Perception & Cognition',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'Which EEG pattern dominates N3 (slow-wave) sleep?',choices:['Theta waves','Delta waves','Beta waves','Alpha waves'],ans:1,exp:'**N3** is characterized by slow, high-amplitude **delta waves** with slow, regular breathing, and it is the deepest stage of non-REM sleep. Theta waves dominate N1, alpha waves mark relaxed wakefulness, and beta waves appear when awake and alert (and resemble REM activity).',src:'aamc-iqb:P10'},
  {s:'Sensation, Perception & Cognition',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'Which feature distinguishes REM sleep from N3 sleep?',choices:['Irregular breathing with skeletal muscle atonia','Slow, regular breathing with high-amplitude waves','Sleep spindles and K-complexes on the EEG','Sleepwalking and night terrors as parasomnias'],ans:0,exp:'**REM sleep** shows fast, desynchronized (beta-like) EEG, rapid eye movements, vivid dreaming, irregular breathing and heart rate, and atonia of skeletal muscles. Sleep spindles and K-complexes mark N2, while sleepwalking and night terrors arise from N3.',src:'aamc-iqb:P10b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'A researcher reinforces one group of pigeons after every 8 pecks and another after every 24 pecks, then counts pecks per hour. The independent variable is:',choices:['the number of pecks made per hour','the type of food used as a reinforcer','the time elapsed between reinforcers','the number of pecks required per reinforcer'],ans:3,exp:'The **independent variable** is the factor the researcher manipulates, here the fixed-ratio requirement (8 vs 24 responses). Pecks per hour is the **dependent variable**, the outcome measured to see the effect of the manipulation.',src:'aamc-iqb:P11'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'A schedule that reinforces the first response made after a set amount of time has passed is a:',choices:['variable-interval schedule','fixed-ratio schedule','fixed-interval schedule','variable-ratio schedule'],ans:2,exp:'**Interval** schedules depend on elapsed time, **ratio** schedules on number of responses; "fixed" means the requirement does not change. Fixed-interval schedules produce a scalloped pattern, with responding rising as the reinforcement time approaches.',src:'aamc-iqb:P11b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'A rat learns to jump over a hurdle whenever a light comes on, which prevents a foot shock. This avoidance behavior is maintained by:',choices:['negative reinforcement','positive reinforcement','negative punishment','positive punishment'],ans:0,exp:'**Negative reinforcement** increases a behavior by removing or preventing an aversive stimulus; escape and avoidance learning are both forms of it. Punishment, by contrast, decreases the behavior it follows.',src:'aamc-iqb:P12'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'Mowrer\'s two-factor theory explains avoidance learning as a combination of:',choices:['operant shaping and classical stimulus extinction','classical fear conditioning and operant fear reduction','observational learning and stimulus habituation','latent learning and spontaneous fear recovery'],ans:1,exp:'In **two-factor theory**, the warning signal first acquires fear through classical conditioning; the avoidance response is then negatively reinforced because it reduces that fear. This explains why avoidance persists even when the shock is no longer delivered.',src:'aamc-iqb:P12b'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7A',q:'Carl Rogers proposed that psychological distress arises mainly from:',choices:['conflict between the id and the superego','behaviors that were never reinforced','incongruence between the real and ideal self','inherited and stable personality traits'],ans:2,exp:'In Rogers\' **humanistic** theory, people strive toward self-actualization, and distress results when the actual self and the ideal self are **incongruent**. Unconditional positive regard from others helps reduce that incongruence; id–superego conflict is a psychodynamic idea.',src:'aamc-iqb:P13'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6C',q:'In Maslow\'s hierarchy of needs, which level sits immediately below esteem needs?',choices:['Safety','Love and belonging','Physiological','Self-actualization'],ans:1,exp:'Maslow\'s levels, from the base, are **physiological, safety, love and belonging, esteem and self-actualization**. Lower needs generally must be met before higher ones become strong motivators; self-actualization sits at the top.',src:'aamc-iqb:P13b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'Rats raised in cages with toys, tunnels and companions develop thicker cortices and more synapses than rats raised alone in bare cages. This best demonstrates:',choices:['Lateralization','Habituation','Myelination','Neural plasticity'],ans:3,exp:'**Neural plasticity** is the brain\'s capacity to change its structure and connections in response to experience, as enriched environments do. Plasticity is greatest early in life but continues into adulthood.',src:'aamc-iqb:P14'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'The lasting strengthening of a synapse after repeated high-frequency stimulation, thought to underlie learning, is called:',choices:['Long-term potentiation','Synaptic pruning','Long-term depression','Habituation'],ans:0,exp:'**Long-term potentiation (LTP)**, studied especially in the hippocampus, increases the postsynaptic response after repeated stimulation (often via NMDA receptors). Long-term depression is the opposite, a lasting weakening of synaptic strength.',src:'aamc-iqb:P14b'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7B',q:'An experienced pianist plays better in front of an audience, while a beginner makes more mistakes when watched. This illustrates:',choices:['Social loafing','Social facilitation','Deindividuation','Bystander effect'],ans:1,exp:'In **social facilitation**, the presence of others raises arousal, which improves performance on simple or well-learned tasks but impairs performance on complex or new ones. Social loafing is reduced effort when individual contributions are pooled in a group.',src:'aamc-iqb:P15'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6C',q:'According to the Yerkes–Dodson law, performance on a difficult, unfamiliar task is best at:',choices:['a very high level of arousal','the lowest possible level of arousal','a moderate-to-low level of arousal','any level, since arousal is irrelevant'],ans:2,exp:'The **Yerkes–Dodson law** describes an inverted-U relation between arousal and performance, with the optimum shifting lower for complex tasks and higher for simple ones. This is why audience-induced arousal helps experts but hurts novices.',src:'aamc-iqb:P15b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'Students who learn material in one lecture hall score higher when tested in that same hall than in a different building. This result supports:',choices:['the serial position effect','proactive interference','the spacing effect','the encoding specificity principle'],ans:3,exp:'The **encoding specificity principle** states that recall is best when cues present at retrieval match those present at encoding, so the matching context is the variable that matters. Context-dependent memory is a direct example.',src:'aamc-iqb:P16'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'A person who memorized a speech while mildly caffeinated recalls it best when caffeinated again. This is an example of:',choices:['State-dependent memory','Mood-congruent memory','Retroactive interference','Implicit memory'],ans:0,exp:'**State-dependent memory** occurs when matching internal physiological or psychological state at encoding and retrieval improves recall. Mood-congruent memory is different: people tend to recall information whose emotional content matches their current mood.',src:'aamc-iqb:P16b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'A typical 9-year-old understands that liquid poured into a taller glass keeps the same volume but struggles with abstract hypothetical problems. Piaget would place this child in the:',choices:['concrete operational stage','formal operational stage','preoperational stage','sensorimotor stage'],ans:0,exp:'The **concrete operational stage** (about 7–11 years) brings conservation and logical reasoning about concrete objects. Abstract and hypothetical reasoning emerges only in the **formal operational stage** (about 12 years and older).',src:'aamc-iqb:P17'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'A 4-year-old insists that a row of 5 spread-out coins contains more coins than a tightly packed row of 5. This error reflects a lack of:',choices:['object permanence','assimilation','conservation','attachment'],ans:2,exp:'**Conservation**, the understanding that quantity is unchanged by changes in appearance, is absent in the preoperational stage (about 2–7 years). Object permanence develops much earlier, during the sensorimotor stage.',src:'aamc-iqb:P17b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'In exposure therapy, a patient with panic disorder remains in a crowded store until the panic fades, without anything bad happening. The weakening of the fear response over repeated sessions reflects:',choices:['negative reinforcement','spontaneous recovery','stimulus generalization','extinction'],ans:3,exp:'Repeatedly presenting the conditioned stimulus without the feared outcome produces **extinction** of the classically conditioned fear response. Leaving the situation early would instead negatively reinforce avoidance by ending the anxiety.',src:'aamc-iqb:P18'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'Weeks after successful exposure therapy, a patient\'s fear briefly returns when she reencounters the feared situation. This is:',choices:['Stimulus discrimination','Spontaneous recovery','Higher-order conditioning','Acquisition'],ans:1,exp:'**Spontaneous recovery** is the reappearance of an extinguished conditioned response after a rest period, showing that extinction suppresses rather than erases the original learning. The recovered response is usually weaker and extinguishes again more quickly.',src:'aamc-iqb:P18b'},
  {s:'Social Psychology',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8B',q:'A counselor helps a student attribute a failed exam to insufficient studying rather than to low ability. This shifts the student\'s attribution toward a cause that is:',choices:['external and uncontrollable','internal and uncontrollable','external and controllable','internal and controllable'],ans:3,exp:'Effort is an **internal, controllable** cause, so the student gains a sense of control over future outcomes, which increases motivation and persistence. Fixed ability is internal but uncontrollable and tends to promote helplessness.',src:'aamc-iqb:P19'},
  {s:'Motivation, Emotion & Personality',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7A',q:'A person who believes that life outcomes are determined mainly by luck and powerful others has:',choices:['an external locus of control','an internal locus of control','high self-efficacy','a growth mindset'],ans:0,exp:'**Locus of control** (Rotter) is the belief about where control over outcomes lies; an external locus attributes it to luck, fate or others. Self-efficacy is a different construct: belief in one\'s ability to perform a specific task.',src:'aamc-iqb:P19b'},
  {s:'Sensation, Perception & Cognition',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6A',q:'To measure the size of a patient\'s lateral ventricles, a researcher should use:',choices:['Electroencephalography','Computed tomography','Positron emission tomography','Electromyography'],ans:1,exp:'**Computed tomography (CT)**, like structural MRI, images anatomical structure such as ventricle size. EEG records electrical activity of the cortex and PET measures metabolic activity, so both reveal function rather than structure.',src:'aamc-iqb:P20'},
  {s:'Sensation, Perception & Cognition',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6A',q:'Which technique maps regional brain glucose metabolism using a radioactive tracer?',choices:['Electroencephalography','Computed tomography','Positron emission tomography','Structural MRI'],ans:2,exp:'**PET** detects positrons from an injected radiolabeled tracer (often fluorodeoxyglucose), showing which regions are metabolically active. Functional MRI measures activity indirectly through the BOLD signal, while CT and structural MRI show anatomy.',src:'aamc-iqb:P20b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'A trainer rewards a dog first for approaching a bell, then only for touching it, and finally only for ringing it. This procedure is:',choices:['Chaining','Habituation','Instinctive drift','Shaping'],ans:3,exp:'**Shaping** reinforces successive approximations of a target behavior until the full behavior appears. Chaining links several already-learned behaviors into a sequence, and instinctive drift is the reversion of trained behavior toward innate patterns.',src:'aamc-iqb:P21'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'A clicker sound that becomes rewarding because it has been repeatedly paired with food is a:',choices:['primary reinforcer','secondary reinforcer','negative reinforcer','discriminative stimulus'],ans:1,exp:'A **secondary (conditioned) reinforcer** acquires value through association with a primary reinforcer such as food, which is inherently rewarding. Money is the classic human example; a discriminative stimulus instead signals that reinforcement is available.',src:'aamc-iqb:P21b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'A patient with severe amnesia cannot recall meeting a nurse each day, yet he is calmer around her than around strangers. This shows impaired _____ memory with preserved _____ memory.',choices:['implicit / explicit','explicit / implicit','procedural / declarative','episodic / semantic'],ans:1,exp:'Conscious recollection of the meetings (**explicit**, declarative memory) is lost, but the unconscious familiarity shown in his behavior reflects intact **implicit** memory. This dissociation is common in Korsakoff syndrome and other hippocampal or diencephalic amnesias.',src:'aamc-iqb:P22'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'Korsakoff syndrome, marked by anterograde amnesia and confabulation, is most often caused by a deficiency of:',choices:['niacin (vitamin B3)','cobalamin (vitamin B12)','folate (vitamin B9)','thiamine (vitamin B1)'],ans:3,exp:'**Thiamine (B1) deficiency**, usually from chronic alcohol use, damages the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus, causing Wernicke encephalopathy and then Korsakoff syndrome. Confabulation fills memory gaps with fabricated details.',src:'aamc-iqb:P22b'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'Participants study a 20-word list and recall it after a 30-second distractor task instead of immediately. Compared with immediate recall, their serial position curve will show:',choices:['a reduced primacy effect but a preserved recency effect','a loss of both the primacy and recency effects','a reduced recency effect but a preserved primacy effect','enhanced primacy and recency effects'],ans:2,exp:'Recency depends on the last items still being held in **short-term memory**, so a distractor-filled delay eliminates it. Primacy depends on extra rehearsal that moved early items into **long-term memory**, so it survives the delay.',src:'aamc-iqb:P23'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6B',q:'The primacy effect in free recall is attributed mainly to:',choices:['items still active in short-term memory','retroactive interference from later items','sensory memory of the first items','greater rehearsal and transfer into long-term memory'],ans:3,exp:'Early list items receive more **rehearsal**, which transfers them into long-term memory and produces the **primacy effect**. The recency effect, by contrast, reflects items still held in short-term memory at the moment of recall.',src:'aamc-iqb:P23b'},
  {s:'Sensation, Perception & Cognition',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6A',q:'Infants shown the same picture repeatedly look at it less and less, but look longer again when a slightly different picture appears. The renewed looking is called:',choices:['Sensitization','Dishabituation','Spontaneous recovery','Accommodation'],ans:1,exp:'**Dishabituation** is the return of a habituated response when the stimulus changes, which shows the infant can perceive the difference between the two stimuli. The habituation–dishabituation paradigm is a standard way to test perception in preverbal infants.',src:'aamc-iqb:P24'},
  {s:'Learning, Memory & Development',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'7C',q:'An increased response to a repeated intense or noxious stimulus, such as flinching more to each successive loud alarm, is called:',choices:['Sensitization','Habituation','Desensitization','Sensory adaptation'],ans:0,exp:'**Sensitization** is a non-associative form of learning in which repeated exposure to a strong or aversive stimulus amplifies the response. Habituation is the opposite, a decreasing response to a repeated, harmless stimulus.',src:'aamc-iqb:P24b'},
  {s:'Sensation, Perception & Cognition',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6A',q:'Which depth cue requires input from both eyes?',choices:['Motion parallax','Linear perspective','Interposition','Retinal disparity'],ans:3,exp:'**Retinal disparity** (the slightly different image on each retina) and **convergence** (inward turning of the eyes for near objects) are binocular cues. Motion parallax, linear perspective, interposition and relative size all work with one eye.',src:'aamc-iqb:P25'},
  {s:'Sensation, Perception & Cognition',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'6A',q:'Riding in a train, a passenger notices that nearby fence posts race past while distant hills barely seem to move. This depth cue is:',choices:['Relative size','Motion parallax','Convergence','Texture gradient'],ans:1,exp:'**Motion parallax** is a monocular cue: when the observer moves, near objects appear to move faster across the visual field than far objects. Convergence is a binocular cue based on how far the eyes turn inward.',src:'aamc-iqb:P25b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'Over several decades, compulsive gambling shifted from being viewed as a moral weakness to a disorder diagnosed and treated by clinicians. This shift is an example of:',choices:['Stigmatization','Deinstitutionalization','Medicalization','Secularization'],ans:2,exp:'**Medicalization** is the process by which behaviors or conditions come to be defined in medical terms and managed by medical professionals (e.g., alcoholism, ADHD, gambling disorder). It can reduce moral blame but also expands medical authority over everyday life.',src:'aamc-iqb:S1'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'According to Talcott Parsons\' concept of the sick role, a sick person is expected to:',choices:['continue all normal role obligations','seek competent help and try to recover','accept blame for having become ill','avoid contact with medical professionals'],ans:1,exp:'Parsons\' **sick role** exempts the sick person from normal duties and from blame for the illness, but in return obliges them to want to get well and to seek technically competent help. It is a functionalist view of illness as a temporary, sanctioned form of deviance.',src:'aamc-iqb:S1b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Which finding most clearly reflects a health disparity?',choices:['Older adults have higher stroke rates than younger adults','Lower-income adults have higher stroke rates than richer adults','Residents at high altitude have higher hemoglobin levels','Women have a lower average risk of gout than men'],ans:1,exp:'A **health disparity** is a difference in health linked to social, economic or environmental disadvantage, such as income or race. Differences driven mainly by age, sex or physiological adaptation are biological variation rather than disparities.',src:'aamc-iqb:S2'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Directing extra outreach resources to a neighborhood with greater need, so that all residents can reach similar health outcomes, reflects a focus on:',choices:['Health equality','Meritocracy','Social reproduction','Health equity'],ans:3,exp:'**Health equity** means distributing resources according to need so that everyone has a fair opportunity to be healthy. Equality means giving everyone the same resources regardless of need, which can leave disparities in place.',src:'aamc-iqb:S2b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'A first-year medical student judges her own study habits by comparing them with those of senior residents she admires, though she is not a resident. The residents serve as her:',choices:['reference group','primary group','in-group','secondary group'],ans:0,exp:'A **reference group** is any group a person uses as a standard to evaluate their own attitudes and behavior, and the person need not belong to it. An in-group is a group one belongs to and identifies with; a primary group is a small, intimate, long-lasting group.',src:'aamc-iqb:S3'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'Which is the best example of a primary group?',choices:['A large university chemistry class','A labor union local','A close-knit nuclear family','Coworkers in a large company'],ans:2,exp:'**Primary groups** are small and intimate, with enduring emotional ties valued for their own sake, like family and close friends. **Secondary groups** are larger, impersonal and goal-oriented, such as classes, unions and workplaces.',src:'aamc-iqb:S3b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'A nurse who is also a single parent must choose between covering an extra shift and attending her child\'s school concert. She is experiencing:',choices:['role strain','role exit','role engulfment','role conflict'],ans:3,exp:'**Role conflict** arises when the demands of two DIFFERENT roles (here, nurse and parent) are incompatible. **Role strain** is tension among competing demands WITHIN a single role.',src:'aamc-iqb:S4'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'A professor feels overwhelmed by the competing demands of teaching, grading and research mentoring that all belong to her single job. She is experiencing:',choices:['role conflict','role strain','role exit','role engulfment'],ans:1,exp:'**Role strain** is stress from incompatible expectations within one role, here the professor role. Role conflict would involve clashes between two separate roles, such as professor and parent.',src:'aamc-iqb:S4b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9B',q:'Which characteristic most directly defines a person\'s ethnicity rather than race?',choices:['Shared language, ancestry and customs','Perceived skin color and facial features','Inherited genetic markers of descent','Legal citizenship in a nation-state'],ans:0,exp:'**Ethnicity** is based on shared culture such as language, heritage, religion and customs, while **race** is a social classification based on perceived physical traits. Both are socially constructed; citizenship refers to nationality, not ethnicity.',src:'aamc-iqb:S5'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9B',q:'Changes over time in which immigrant groups were counted as "white" in the United States best support the view that race is:',choices:['biologically fixed','equivalent to nationality','determined by ancestry alone','socially constructed'],ans:3,exp:'Because racial boundaries shift with history, law and politics, race is understood as **socially constructed** rather than a fixed biological category. **Racialization** is the process of assigning racial meaning to a group.',src:'aamc-iqb:S5b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'A traveler calls a host culture\'s custom of eating with the hands primitive simply because it differs from her own norms. This illustrates:',choices:['Cultural relativism','Culture shock','Ethnocentrism','Cultural diffusion'],ans:2,exp:'**Ethnocentrism** is judging other cultures by the standards of one\'s own, treating one\'s own norms as superior or universal. Culture shock is the disorientation felt on entering an unfamiliar culture, not a judgment of its worth.',src:'aamc-iqb:S6'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'An anthropologist interprets a community\'s healing ritual in light of that community\'s beliefs and values rather than her own. She is practicing:',choices:['Ethnocentrism','Cultural relativism','Cultural assimilation','Xenocentrism'],ans:1,exp:'**Cultural relativism** seeks to understand a culture on its own terms, the opposite of ethnocentrism. Xenocentrism is the preference for other cultures over one\'s own.',src:'aamc-iqb:S6b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8B',q:'Goffman\'s term for an attribute that deeply discredits a person, reducing them in others\' eyes from a whole person to a tainted one, is:',choices:['Anomie','Deviance','Prejudice','Stigma'],ans:3,exp:'**Stigma** is a socially devalued attribute (e.g., a mental illness diagnosis or HIV status) that spoils a person\'s identity. Stigma can discourage people from seeking care and can be internalized as self-stigma.',src:'aamc-iqb:S7'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'A landlord refuses to rent an apartment to an applicant after learning she has a psychiatric diagnosis. The landlord\'s action is an example of:',choices:['discrimination','prejudice','stereotyping','ethnocentrism'],ans:0,exp:'**Discrimination** is a behavior: unequal treatment of people based on group membership. Prejudice is the negative attitude or feeling, and a stereotype is the oversimplified belief, so the refusal is the action that may follow from both.',src:'aamc-iqb:S7b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'During rapid industrialization, many workers report that shared rules no longer guide their lives and feel disconnected from society. Durkheim called this condition:',choices:['Alienation','Rationalization','Anomie','Social stratification'],ans:2,exp:'**Anomie** (Durkheim) is normlessness: a breakdown of shared norms and social bonds, often during rapid social change, linked to deviance and suicide. Alienation is Marx\'s concept of workers\' estrangement from their labor and its products.',src:'aamc-iqb:S8'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'Merton\'s strain theory predicts that deviance increases when a society:',choices:['labels some members as outsiders','teaches deviant norms through close peers','stresses goals but limits legitimate means','strongly enforces informal social controls'],ans:2,exp:'**Strain theory** extends anomie: when culture emphasizes goals like wealth but blocks legitimate means to them, some people respond with innovation (illegitimate means), ritualism, retreatism or rebellion. Labeling and peer transmission describe labeling theory and differential association.',src:'aamc-iqb:S8b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8A',q:'A teenager begins to see herself as funny after noticing that classmates seem to enjoy her jokes. Cooley\'s term for this process is:',choices:['the generalized other','the looking-glass self','self-efficacy','social comparison'],ans:1,exp:'In Cooley\'s **looking-glass self**, self-concept develops from imagining how we appear to others and how they judge us. Social comparison involves evaluating oneself against others\' abilities, not imagining their judgments of oneself.',src:'aamc-iqb:S9'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8A',q:'In George Herbert Mead\'s theory, the part of the self that reflects internalized social expectations and attitudes of others is the:',choices:['"I"','ideal self','"me"','ego'],ans:2,exp:'Mead\'s **"me"** is the socialized self, built by taking the perspective of the **generalized other**, while the **"I"** is the spontaneous, impulsive response to the present moment. Both develop through social interaction, in stages of play and game.',src:'aamc-iqb:S9b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Which study design would best test the life course approach to health?',choices:['Correlating adults\' current jobs with their current incomes','Comparing hospital admission rates across states in one year','Following children into adulthood to link early adversity to later disease','Surveying adults about current diet and blood pressure'],ans:2,exp:'The **life course approach** holds that exposures across earlier life stages (childhood health, adolescent stress, learned behaviors) shape adult health, so it requires longitudinal data linking early conditions to later outcomes. Cross-sectional adult correlations cannot test it.',src:'aamc-iqb:S10'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Findings that adverse childhood experiences predict adult heart disease and depression support the idea that social disadvantage:',choices:['affects only childhood health','is fully reversed by adolescence','matters mainly in old age','accumulates across the life course'],ans:3,exp:'**Cumulative disadvantage** describes how early adversity compounds over time, through biological stress responses, behaviors and reduced opportunities, to shape adult health. This is a core idea of the life course perspective.',src:'aamc-iqb:S10b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'A city taxes sugary drinks and subsidizes fresh fruit, expecting residents to buy more fruit as relative prices change. Which theory underlies this policy?',choices:['Rational choice theory','Symbolic interactionism','Conflict theory','Social constructionism'],ans:0,exp:'**Rational choice theory** assumes people weigh costs and benefits to maximize their self-interest, so altering prices or incentives should alter behavior. Symbolic interactionism would instead focus on the meanings people attach to foods.',src:'aamc-iqb:S11'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'Social exchange theory explains why people stay in a relationship mainly in terms of:',choices:['shared symbols and meanings','struggles over scarce resources','contributions to social stability','perceived rewards outweighing costs'],ans:3,exp:'**Social exchange theory** applies cost–benefit reasoning to interactions: people maintain relationships whose perceived rewards exceed their costs. Struggles over resources and contributions to stability describe conflict theory and functionalism.',src:'aamc-iqb:S11b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9B',q:'Which male–female difference is best explained by gender rather than sex?',choices:['Higher injury rates linked to norms rewarding risk-taking','Earlier average onset of puberty in girls','Higher circulating testosterone levels in men','Greater osteoporosis risk in postmenopausal women'],ans:0,exp:'**Gender** refers to socially constructed roles, norms and behaviors, so injury differences driven by masculine risk-taking norms reflect gender. **Sex** refers to biological traits such as hormones, puberty timing and bone density after menopause.',src:'aamc-iqb:S12'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9B',q:'A person\'s internal sense of being male, female, both or neither is their:',choices:['biological sex','sexual orientation','gender role','gender identity'],ans:3,exp:'**Gender identity** is one\'s internal sense of gender, which may or may not match sex assigned at birth. Sexual orientation concerns whom one is attracted to, and gender roles are a society\'s expected behaviors for each gender.',src:'aamc-iqb:S12b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'A researcher argues that adolescents vape mainly because the activity signals belonging to a valued peer group. This explanation reflects:',choices:['Functionalism','Symbolic interactionism','Conflict theory','Rational choice theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Symbolic interactionism** focuses on the shared meanings that people attach to symbols and behaviors in everyday interaction, such as a behavior signaling group identity. Functionalism asks what a behavior does for society\'s stability, and conflict theory focuses on power and inequality.',src:'aamc-iqb:S13'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'Explaining the concentration of tobacco advertising in poor neighborhoods as powerful corporations profiting from disadvantaged groups reflects:',choices:['functionalism','symbolic interactionism','conflict theory','social exchange theory'],ans:2,exp:'**Conflict theory** (rooted in Marx) views society as a struggle over scarce resources in which powerful groups exploit less powerful ones. It explains health patterns through inequality and power rather than shared meanings or social functions.',src:'aamc-iqb:S13b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Older adults with many close friendships and active community ties show lower levels of inflammatory markers. These networks of relationships are a form of:',choices:['cultural capital','human capital','economic capital','social capital'],ans:3,exp:'**Social capital** is the resources available through social networks and relationships, such as support, information and trust; it is associated with better health outcomes. Cultural capital refers to knowledge, skills and credentials that confer advantage.',src:'aamc-iqb:S14'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Knowledge, tastes and educational credentials that help a person navigate institutions such as schools are called:',choices:['cultural capital','social capital','human capital','economic capital'],ans:0,exp:'Bourdieu\'s **cultural capital** consists of non-financial assets such as education, language and tastes that are valued by dominant institutions. Unequal cultural capital helps explain **social reproduction**, the passing of class position across generations.',src:'aamc-iqb:S14b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'Students who are publicly identified by teachers as "troublemakers" begin to act out more over the following year. Which theory best predicts this?',choices:['Differential association theory','Labeling theory','Strain theory','Social control theory'],ans:1,exp:'**Labeling theory** holds that deviance results from the labels society applies; a labeled person may be treated as deviant and internalize the label, leading to **secondary deviance**. Differential association emphasizes learning deviance from close associates.',src:'aamc-iqb:S15'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'A teenager learns to shoplift from friends who teach him techniques and attitudes that approve of stealing. This illustrates:',choices:['labeling theory','general strain theory','social control theory','differential association theory'],ans:3,exp:'Sutherland\'s **differential association theory** proposes that deviance is learned through interaction with others who define it favorably, including techniques and motives. Labeling theory instead attributes deviance to social reactions, and strain theory links it to blocked access to legitimate means.',src:'aamc-iqb:S15b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'A condition that one society classifies as a mental disorder is regarded in another society as a normal spiritual experience. This best supports:',choices:['biological determinism','functionalism','social constructionism','rational choice theory'],ans:2,exp:'**Social constructionism** holds that categories such as mental illness are created and defined through social processes, so they vary across cultures and over time. Biological determinism would predict the same classification everywhere.',src:'aamc-iqb:S16'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9A',q:'The removal of homosexuality as a diagnosis from the DSM in 1973 is often cited as evidence that psychiatric categories are partly:',choices:['socially constructed','genetically determined','universally fixed','statistically derived'],ans:0,exp:'Changes in diagnostic categories that follow shifts in social values, rather than new biological evidence, illustrate the **social construction** of illness. Such changes also show how medicalization can be reversed (demedicalization).',src:'aamc-iqb:S16b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9B',q:'In the demographic transition model, which change typically occurs FIRST as a country industrializes?',choices:['Birth rates fall','Death rates fall','Birth rates rise','Death rates rise'],ans:1,exp:'In the **demographic transition**, improved sanitation, nutrition and medicine first lower **death rates**; birth rates fall later as urbanization, education and contraception spread. Societies move from high birth and death rates to low birth and death rates.',src:'aamc-iqb:S17'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'9B',q:'During which phase of the demographic transition does population grow most rapidly?',choices:['When birth and death rates are both still high','When birth and death rates have both become low','When birth rates have fallen below death rates','When death rates have fallen but birth rates remain high'],ans:3,exp:'The gap between a falling **death rate** and a still-high **birth rate** (stage 2) produces rapid natural increase. Before and after the transition, births and deaths are roughly balanced, so growth is slow.',src:'aamc-iqb:S17b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'A field that studies how income, neighborhood conditions and racism shape the distribution of disease in populations is:',choices:['Social epidemiology','Clinical epidemiology','Demography','Biostatistics'],ans:0,exp:'**Social epidemiology** examines the social determinants of health, such as socioeconomic status, race, neighborhood and social networks. Demography studies population size, structure and change through births, deaths and migration.',src:'aamc-iqb:S18'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'The number of NEW cases of a disease in a population during a specified period is its:',choices:['prevalence','mortality rate','incidence','case fatality rate'],ans:2,exp:'**Incidence** counts new cases over a period, while **prevalence** counts all existing cases (new and old) at a point or over a period. A long-lasting chronic disease can have a low incidence but a high prevalence.',src:'aamc-iqb:S18b'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'A physician speaks formally with patients but jokes casually with colleagues in the staff lounge, out of patients\' view. Goffman would call the staff lounge:',choices:['front stage','the generalized other','a reference group','back stage'],ans:3,exp:'In Goffman\'s **dramaturgical approach**, people perform for audiences on the **front stage** and relax out of the audience\'s view on the **back stage**. Adjusting one\'s presentation to the audience is impression management.',src:'aamc-iqb:S19'},
  {s:'Sociology & Social Structure',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'8C',q:'Highlighting one\'s research awards during a residency interview to appear competent is an example of:',choices:['role conflict','impression management','cognitive dissonance','social loafing'],ans:1,exp:'**Impression management** is the deliberate effort to shape how others perceive us, using strategies such as self-promotion, ingratiation and managing appearance. It is the central process in Goffman\'s dramaturgical approach.',src:'aamc-iqb:S19b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Which is an essential ethical requirement for research with human participants?',choices:['Participants must receive monetary payment','Participants may withdraw at any time without penalty','All participants must answer identical questions','Participants must be told the hypothesis beforehand'],ans:1,exp:'Core protections include **informed consent**, the **right to withdraw** without penalty, confidentiality and minimizing harm. Payment is optional, and revealing the hypothesis is not required (it can bias results) as long as consent is informed.',src:'aamc-iqb:S20'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'When an approved study uses deception, ethical guidelines require researchers to:',choices:['debrief participants promptly afterward','reveal the deception only if asked','pay participants additional compensation','skip the informed consent process'],ans:0,exp:'Deception is permitted only when necessary and approved by an IRB, and participants must be **debriefed** afterward, told the true purpose and given the chance to withdraw their data. Informed consent is still required for the parts of the study that can be disclosed.',src:'aamc-iqb:S20b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'A study uses whether a household receives food assistance as its only measure of socioeconomic status. This choice mainly raises a concern about:',choices:['reliability','validity','statistical power','random assignment'],ans:1,exp:'**Validity** asks whether a measure captures what it is meant to measure; receipt of aid misses many dimensions of SES such as education, occupation and wealth. **Reliability** is consistency of a measure across repeated use.',src:'aamc-iqb:S21'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'A depression scale gives very different scores when the same patients with stable symptoms complete it twice, one week apart. The scale lacks:',choices:['construct validity','internal validity','test–retest reliability','external validity'],ans:2,exp:'**Test–retest reliability** is the consistency of scores over time when the underlying trait is stable. A measure can be reliable without being valid, but it cannot be valid if it is unreliable.',src:'aamc-iqb:S21b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'A sociologist spends a year shadowing ICU physicians to understand how they manage conflicting demands in their daily work. This research method is:',choices:['an experiment','a cross-sectional survey','a comparative study','ethnography'],ans:3,exp:'**Ethnography** studies a group in its natural setting, often through extended participant observation, to understand its practices and meanings from the inside. It yields rich qualitative data but limited generalizability and no causal control.',src:'aamc-iqb:S22'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Factory workers increase their productivity simply because they know researchers are observing them. This is called:',choices:['the placebo effect','the Hawthorne effect','observer bias','the Pygmalion effect'],ans:1,exp:'The **Hawthorne effect** is a change in participants\' behavior caused by awareness of being studied, a threat to validity in observational research. Observer bias refers to the researcher\'s expectations distorting what is recorded.',src:'aamc-iqb:S22b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Recent immigrants often have better health than U.S.-born members of the same ethnic group, despite lower average income. This pattern is known as:',choices:['the healthy worker effect','the immigrant health paradox','the socioeconomic gradient','the social drift hypothesis'],ans:1,exp:'The **immigrant health paradox** describes better health among first-generation immigrants than expected from their SES and than among U.S.-born peers. Proposed explanations include protective cultural practices and the selective migration of healthier people.',src:'aamc-iqb:S23'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Which observation is most consistent with the immigrant health paradox?',choices:['Health tends to decline with more years in the host country','Health improves steadily as income rises after arrival','Immigrants have worse health than U.S.-born peers on arrival','Second-generation children show the best health outcomes'],ans:0,exp:'As immigrants **acculturate**, adopting host-country diet, smoking and stress patterns, their health advantage tends to erode, so greater assimilation correlates with worse health. This decline is why the advantage is strongest among recent arrivals.',src:'aamc-iqb:S23b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'For a sample of household incomes that includes a few extremely wealthy households, which statistic best represents a typical household?',choices:['Mean','Mode','Median','Midrange'],ans:2,exp:'The **median** is resistant to outliers, whereas the mean is pulled toward the long tail of high incomes. Income distributions are right-skewed, so the mean exceeds the median.',src:'aamc-iqb:S24'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'In a right (positively) skewed distribution, the measures of central tendency typically fall in which order, from lowest to highest value?',choices:['mean, median, mode','median, mode, mean','mean, mode, median','mode, median, mean'],ans:3,exp:'In a **positively skewed** distribution, the tail of high values pulls the mean upward, so typically mode < median < mean. In a negatively skewed distribution the order is reversed.',src:'aamc-iqb:S24b'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'Which pattern best describes the socioeconomic gradient in health?',choices:['Risk falls stepwise with each higher level of SES','Only the poorest group has elevated disease risk','Disease risk is lowest among middle-income groups','SES affects health only below a poverty threshold'],ans:0,exp:'The **socioeconomic gradient** means health improves at every step up the SES ladder, not just above a poverty line, so education and income correlate negatively with disease risk across the whole range. The Whitehall studies of civil servants are a classic demonstration.',src:'aamc-iqb:S25'},
  {s:'Health, Inequality & Research Methods',disc:'psych_soc',aamc:'10A',q:'The hypothesis that people with schizophrenia move into lower socioeconomic positions because the illness impairs their ability to work is called:',choices:['the social causation hypothesis','the social drift hypothesis','the immigrant health paradox','the life course approach'],ans:1,exp:'**Social drift** (selection) explains the SES–illness association as illness causing downward mobility. **Social causation** argues the reverse, that the stresses of low SES cause illness.',src:'aamc-iqb:S25b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5C',q:'Two proteins each have a mass of 120 kDa and the same pI; one is a single chain, the other is two 60 kDa chains joined by a disulfide bond. Which method separates them?',choices:['Isoelectric focusing','Nonreducing SDS-PAGE','Reducing SDS-PAGE','Ion-exchange chromatography'],ans:2,exp:'A reducing agent such as β-mercaptoethanol or DTT breaks the disulfide, so in **reducing SDS-PAGE** the dimer runs as a 60 kDa band while the single chain runs at 120 kDa. Isoelectric focusing and ion exchange separate by charge, which is identical here.',src:'aamc-iqb:B1'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5C',q:'Isoelectric focusing separates proteins on the basis of their:',choices:['molecular mass','isoelectric point','hydrodynamic size','hydrophobicity'],ans:1,exp:'In **isoelectric focusing**, proteins migrate through a pH gradient until they reach the pH where their net charge is zero (their **pI**), then stop. SDS-PAGE separates by mass, and native PAGE by size, shape and charge.',src:'aamc-iqb:B1b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'An inhibitor raises the apparent Km of an enzyme but leaves Vmax unchanged. The inhibitor is most likely:',choices:['uncompetitive','noncompetitive','irreversible','competitive'],ans:3,exp:'A **competitive** inhibitor binds the active site, competing with substrate, so more substrate is needed to reach half-maximal velocity (higher apparent Km). Because high [S] outcompetes it, Vmax is unchanged.',src:'aamc-iqb:B2'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'On a Lineweaver–Burk plot, the lines obtained with and without a competitive inhibitor intersect:',choices:['on the y-axis','on the x-axis','to the left of the y-axis','nowhere, because they are parallel'],ans:0,exp:'A competitive inhibitor leaves Vmax (and thus the **y-intercept, 1/Vmax**) unchanged while increasing the slope, so the lines cross on the y-axis. Noncompetitive inhibitors intersect on the x-axis, and uncompetitive inhibitors give parallel lines.',src:'aamc-iqb:B2b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2A',q:'A hydropathy plot of a membrane protein shows four separate stretches of about 20 consecutive strongly hydrophobic residues. The protein most likely has:',choices:['two transmembrane α-helices','four transmembrane α-helices','eight transmembrane α-helices','four cytosolic β-sheets'],ans:1,exp:'Each sustained peak of about **20 hydrophobic residues** on a hydropathy plot predicts one membrane-spanning **α-helix**, so four peaks predict four transmembrane segments. Hydrophilic stretches between peaks correspond to loops in the aqueous compartments.',src:'aamc-iqb:B3'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2A',q:'About how many amino acids are needed for an α-helix to span the roughly 30 Å hydrophobic core of a lipid bilayer?',choices:['About 5','About 50','About 20','About 100'],ans:2,exp:'An α-helix rises about **1.5 Å per residue**, so about 20 residues span 30 Å. This is why hydropathy analysis looks for windows of roughly 20 hydrophobic amino acids.',src:'aamc-iqb:B3b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'In a Michaelis–Menten experiment, which quantity is the dependent variable?',choices:['Substrate concentration','Enzyme concentration','Initial reaction velocity','Reaction temperature'],ans:2,exp:'The experimenter sets **substrate concentration** (the independent variable) and measures **initial velocity, v0** (the dependent variable), holding enzyme concentration and temperature constant. Plotting v0 against [S] gives the hyperbola used to find Km and Vmax.',src:'aamc-iqb:B4'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Why are initial velocities used when collecting Michaelis–Menten data?',choices:['Substrate depletion and reverse reaction are negligible','The enzyme is always saturated at early times','Product inhibition is greatest at early times','The enzyme denatures after the first minute'],ans:0,exp:'Measuring **v0** before much product forms keeps [S] essentially equal to its starting value and makes the reverse reaction and product inhibition negligible. This satisfies the assumptions behind the Michaelis–Menten equation.',src:'aamc-iqb:B4b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1B',q:'Which 60-base-pair DNA duplex would have the highest melting temperature?',choices:['One with 30% G–C content','One with 50% G–C content','One with 10% G–C content','One with 70% G–C content'],ans:3,exp:'Melting temperature rises with **G–C content** because each G–C pair forms three hydrogen bonds (versus two for A–T) and G–C-rich sequences have stronger base stacking. Length and salt concentration also raise Tm, but length is constant here.',src:'aamc-iqb:B5'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1B',q:'As double-stranded DNA is heated and denatures, its absorbance at 260 nm:',choices:['decreases','increases','stays constant','shifts to 280 nm'],ans:1,exp:'Unstacked bases in single strands absorb more UV light than stacked bases in the duplex, the **hyperchromic effect**. Tracking A260 during heating gives the melting curve, whose midpoint is Tm.',src:'aamc-iqb:B5b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'How many NADH are produced by glycolysis from 5 molecules of glucose?',choices:['5','10','20','15'],ans:1,exp:'Glycolysis yields **2 NADH**, 2 pyruvate and a net 2 ATP per glucose, so 5 glucose give 10 NADH. In aerobic cells this cytosolic NADH is shuttled into mitochondria; in anaerobic cells it is reoxidized by lactate formation.',src:'aamc-iqb:B6'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Which glycolytic enzyme generates NADH?',choices:['Phosphoglycerate kinase','Phosphofructokinase-1','Phosphoglucose isomerase','Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase'],ans:3,exp:'**Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase** oxidizes G3P to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate, reducing NAD⁺ to NADH. Because it needs NAD⁺, glycolysis stalls unless NADH is reoxidized (by the electron transport chain or fermentation).',src:'aamc-iqb:B6b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2A',q:'Which membrane would be the most fluid?',choices:['Unsaturated acyl chains at 40 °C','Saturated acyl chains at 40 °C','Unsaturated acyl chains at 10 °C','Saturated acyl chains at 10 °C'],ans:0,exp:'**Cis double bonds** kink acyl chains and prevent tight packing, and **higher temperature** adds kinetic energy, so both increase fluidity. Saturated chains at low temperature pack most tightly and form the most ordered membrane.',src:'aamc-iqb:B7'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2A',q:'At high temperatures, cholesterol in an animal cell membrane:',choices:['increases fluidity by disrupting packing','decreases fluidity by restraining phospholipids','has no effect on fluidity','increases fluidity by adding double bonds'],ans:1,exp:'Cholesterol is a **fluidity buffer**: at high temperature its rigid rings restrain phospholipid movement, decreasing fluidity, while at low temperature it disrupts tight packing, preventing the membrane from becoming too rigid.',src:'aamc-iqb:B7b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Standard free energies of hydrolysis are −61.9 kJ/mol for phosphoenolpyruvate, −30.5 kJ/mol for ATP and −13.8 kJ/mol for glucose-6-phosphate. Which phosphoryl transfer is spontaneous under standard conditions?',choices:['Glucose-6-phosphate donating to ADP','ATP donating to pyruvate to form PEP','Glucose-6-phosphate donating to pyruvate','PEP donating to ADP to form ATP'],ans:3,exp:'A compound with a more negative ΔG°\' of hydrolysis has higher **phosphoryl-transfer potential** and can spontaneously phosphorylate the hydrolysis product of a compound with lower potential. The ranking is PEP > ATP > glucose-6-phosphate, so PEP can phosphorylate ADP (as in the pyruvate kinase reaction).',src:'aamc-iqb:B8'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Using ΔG°\' of hydrolysis values of −61.9 kJ/mol for PEP and −30.5 kJ/mol for ATP, what is ΔG°\' for PEP + ADP → pyruvate + ATP?',choices:['+31.4 kJ/mol','−92.4 kJ/mol','−31.4 kJ/mol','−48.1 kJ/mol'],ans:2,exp:'Add PEP hydrolysis (−61.9) to the reverse of ATP hydrolysis (+30.5): **−61.9 + 30.5 = −31.4 kJ/mol**. Coupling reactions this way is how cells drive unfavorable steps, and the large negative value makes pyruvate kinase essentially irreversible.',src:'aamc-iqb:B8b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'3A',q:'When a hormone binds a G protein–coupled receptor, the activated receptor directly causes:',choices:['the Gα subunit to hydrolyze GTP to GDP','the Gα subunit to exchange GDP for GTP','adenylyl cyclase to phosphorylate the receptor','the Gβγ dimer to bind GTP'],ans:1,exp:'The ligand-bound receptor acts as a **guanine nucleotide exchange factor (GEF)**, causing Gα to release GDP and bind GTP, after which Gα–GTP dissociates from Gβγ and activates effectors. The intrinsic **GTPase** activity of Gα later hydrolyzes GTP to end the signal.',src:'aamc-iqb:B9'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'3A',q:'Cholera toxin causes persistently high cAMP in intestinal cells by:',choices:['blocking the GTPase activity of Gαs','stimulating the GTPase activity of Gαs','inhibiting adenylyl cyclase directly','activating cAMP phosphodiesterase'],ans:0,exp:'Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates **Gαs**, blocking its GTPase so it stays GTP-bound and keeps adenylyl cyclase active; the resulting cAMP opens CFTR chloride channels, causing watery diarrhea. Pertussis toxin, by contrast, inactivates Gαi.',src:'aamc-iqb:B9b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2C',q:'Which feature distinguishes apoptosis from necrosis?',choices:['The cell swells and bursts, spilling its contents','Released enzymes damage neighboring cells','Cell fragments are cleared without causing inflammation','The plasma membrane ruptures early in the process'],ans:2,exp:'In **apoptosis**, the cell shrinks, blebs and breaks into membrane-bound apoptotic bodies that phagocytes engulf **without inflammation**. In necrosis, cells swell and lyse, releasing contents that injure neighbors and provoke inflammation.',src:'aamc-iqb:B10'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2C',q:'Release of which mitochondrial protein triggers the intrinsic apoptotic pathway by promoting apoptosome formation and caspase-9 activation?',choices:['Coenzyme Q','ATP synthase','Cardiolipin','Cytochrome c'],ans:3,exp:'When pro-apoptotic Bcl-2 family proteins (Bax, Bak) permeabilize the outer mitochondrial membrane, **cytochrome c** enters the cytosol, binds Apaf-1 to form the apoptosome and activates caspase-9, which activates executioner caspases. Anti-apoptotic Bcl-2 blocks this release.',src:'aamc-iqb:B10b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2A',q:'Which transporter carries out primary active transport?',choices:['Aquaporin','Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase','Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger','Voltage-gated K⁺ channel'],ans:1,exp:'**Primary active transport** uses ATP hydrolysis directly; the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per ATP. Channels and aquaporins are passive, and the red-cell Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger is a passive antiporter driven by existing gradients.',src:'aamc-iqb:B11'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'2A',q:'The Na⁺–glucose cotransporter of intestinal cells moves glucose against its concentration gradient by using:',choices:['direct hydrolysis of ATP by the cotransporter','facilitated diffusion down the glucose gradient','a proton gradient made by the electron transport chain','the Na⁺ gradient made by the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase'],ans:3,exp:'**Secondary active transport** couples uphill movement of one solute to downhill movement of another, here Na⁺ entering down the gradient that the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase maintains. The cotransporter itself does not hydrolyze ATP.',src:'aamc-iqb:B11b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Which molecule is NOT an intermediate of the citric acid cycle?',choices:['Pyruvate','Isocitrate','Fumarate','Succinyl-CoA'],ans:0,exp:'The cycle\'s intermediates are citrate, isocitrate, α-ketoglutarate, succinyl-CoA, succinate, fumarate, malate and oxaloacetate. **Pyruvate** is a glycolytic product that must first be converted to acetyl-CoA before its carbons enter the cycle.',src:'aamc-iqb:B12'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Pyruvate is converted to acetyl-CoA for entry into the citric acid cycle by which enzyme complex?',choices:['Pyruvate carboxylase','Pyruvate dehydrogenase','Pyruvate kinase','Lactate dehydrogenase'],ans:1,exp:'The mitochondrial **pyruvate dehydrogenase complex** oxidatively decarboxylates pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, producing CO₂ and NADH and requiring thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoate, CoA, FAD and NAD⁺. Pyruvate carboxylase instead makes oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis or anaplerosis.',src:'aamc-iqb:B12b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Which amino acid has the highest isoelectric point?',choices:['Lysine','Histidine','Arginine','Glutamate'],ans:2,exp:'**Arginine** has the most basic side chain (guanidinium pKa about 12.5), giving it the highest pI (about 10.8), just above lysine (about 9.7). Acidic residues such as glutamate and aspartate have the lowest pI values, around 3.',src:'aamc-iqb:B13'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Which amino acid has a side-chain pKa near 6, making it well suited to act as a general acid or base in enzyme active sites at physiological pH?',choices:['Lysine','Arginine','Aspartate','Histidine'],ans:3,exp:'The **histidine** imidazole side chain (pKa about 6) can readily gain or lose a proton near pH 7, so it frequently serves in acid–base catalysis, as in the catalytic triad of serine proteases. Lysine and arginine are almost always protonated, and aspartate almost always deprotonated, at pH 7.',src:'aamc-iqb:B13b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'A glutamate side chain in a binding pocket is best stabilized by a hydrogen bond from which neighboring side chain?',choices:['Aspartate','Glutamine','Valine','Alanine'],ans:1,exp:'Glutamate\'s carboxylate is a **hydrogen-bond acceptor**, so it pairs well with a donor such as glutamine\'s amide N–H. Aspartate is also negatively charged and would repel it, and valine and alanine are nonpolar.',src:'aamc-iqb:B14'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'At pH 7, which amino acid side chain can act ONLY as a hydrogen-bond acceptor, not a donor?',choices:['Serine','Asparagine','Tyrosine','Aspartate'],ans:3,exp:'At pH 7 aspartate\'s side chain is a deprotonated **carboxylate** with lone pairs but no O–H, so it can only accept hydrogen bonds. Serine and tyrosine (–OH) and asparagine (amide N–H and C=O) can both donate and accept.',src:'aamc-iqb:B14b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'An uncompetitive inhibitor binds only the enzyme–substrate complex. How does it change Vmax and Km?',choices:['Both decrease by the same factor','Vmax decreases while Km is unchanged','Km increases while Vmax is unchanged','Both increase by the same factor'],ans:0,exp:'By binding ES, an **uncompetitive** inhibitor pulls the equilibrium toward ES (lowering apparent Km) while reducing catalytic turnover (lowering Vmax) by the same factor. Km/Vmax, the Lineweaver–Burk slope, is unchanged, so the lines are parallel.',src:'aamc-iqb:B15'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'On a Lineweaver–Burk plot, lines with and without a pure noncompetitive inhibitor:',choices:['intersect on the y-axis','are parallel to each other','intersect on the x-axis','intersect in the first quadrant'],ans:2,exp:'A **pure noncompetitive** inhibitor binds E and ES equally, lowering Vmax (raising 1/Vmax) without changing Km, so the lines share the x-intercept, −1/Km. Mixed inhibitors that prefer E or ES intersect off the axes, to the left of the y-axis.',src:'aamc-iqb:B15b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'An enzyme\'s initial velocity plateaus at 250 μM/min and reaches 125 μM/min when [S] = 25 μM. What is the slope of its Lineweaver–Burk plot?',choices:['10 min','0.1 min','0.01 min','1 min'],ans:1,exp:'**Vmax** is the plateau (250 μM/min) and **Km** is [S] at half-Vmax (25 μM); the Lineweaver–Burk slope is Km/Vmax = 25 μM ÷ 250 μM/min = 0.1 min. Inverting the ratio, which gives 10 min, is the common error.',src:'aamc-iqb:B16'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'On a Lineweaver–Burk (1/v0 versus 1/[S]) plot, the x-intercept equals:',choices:['1/Vmax','Km/Vmax','−Km','−1/Km'],ans:3,exp:'The double-reciprocal equation is 1/v0 = (Km/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax, so the **y-intercept is 1/Vmax**, the **slope is Km/Vmax** and the **x-intercept is −1/Km**.',src:'aamc-iqb:B16b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Which ligand binds its receptor with the highest affinity?',choices:['One with Kd = 5 μM','One with Kd = 0.5 mM','One with Kd = 5 nM','One with Kd = 50 μM'],ans:2,exp:'The **dissociation constant (Kd)** is the ligand concentration at which half the binding sites are occupied, so a lower Kd means tighter binding and higher affinity. 5 nM is a thousand-fold lower than 5 μM.',src:'aamc-iqb:B17'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'A receptor has a Kd of 40 nM for its ligand. What fraction of receptors is occupied when the free ligand concentration is 40 nM?',choices:['50%','40%','90%','100%'],ans:0,exp:'Fractional occupancy = [L] / ([L] + Kd) = 40 / (40 + 40) = **0.5**. By definition, Kd equals the free ligand concentration that gives half-maximal binding.',src:'aamc-iqb:B17b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'During one purification step, total activity falls from 1000 to 600 units while total protein falls from 500 mg to 10 mg. The specific activity:',choices:['decreases 30-fold','increases 1.7-fold','increases 30-fold','is unchanged'],ans:2,exp:'**Specific activity** is activity per mg of total protein: 1000/500 = 2 units/mg before and 600/10 = 60 units/mg after, a 30-fold increase. Rising specific activity shows that contaminating proteins are being removed.',src:'aamc-iqb:B18'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'If total enzyme activity falls from 2000 to 500 units during a purification step, the yield for that step is:',choices:['75%','40%','4%','25%'],ans:3,exp:'**Yield** is the fraction of starting total activity retained: 500/2000 = 25%. A good purification step trades some yield for a large gain in specific activity (purification fold).',src:'aamc-iqb:B18b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5C',q:'A mixture of a 500 kDa protein complex, a 60 kDa protein and urea is applied to a size-exclusion column. Which elutes first?',choices:['The 60 kDa protein','The 500 kDa complex','Urea','All three together'],ans:1,exp:'In **size-exclusion chromatography**, large molecules are excluded from the bead pores and travel the shortest path, so they elute first; small molecules such as urea enter all pores and elute last. This is the opposite of SDS-PAGE, where small proteins move farthest.',src:'aamc-iqb:B19'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5C',q:'Proteins bound to a cation-exchange column are most commonly eluted by:',choices:['increasing the salt concentration','decreasing the salt concentration','adding a reducing agent','lowering the temperature'],ans:0,exp:'Rising **salt concentration** supplies cations that compete with bound proteins for the negatively charged resin, releasing the proteins (raising the pH so proteins lose positive charge also works). A **cation exchanger** binds positively charged proteins, those below their pI.',src:'aamc-iqb:B19b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Compared with saturated stearic acid (18:0), linolenic acid (18:3) has how many fewer hydrogen atoms?',choices:['3','2','12','6'],ans:3,exp:'Each **C=C double bond** removes 2 hydrogen atoms, so three double bonds remove 6: stearic acid is C₁₈H₃₆O₂ and linolenic acid is C₁₈H₃₀O₂. This is why unsaturated fats are described as not "saturated" with hydrogen.',src:'aamc-iqb:B20'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Adding cis double bonds to a fatty acid chain of fixed length does what to its melting point?',choices:['Raises it by adding hydrogen bonding','Lowers it by disrupting chain packing','Raises it by increasing chain packing','Leaves it essentially unchanged'],ans:1,exp:'**Cis double bonds** put kinks in the chain that prevent tight packing and weaken van der Waals interactions, lowering the melting point; this is why unsaturated oils are liquid at room temperature. Longer chains, by contrast, raise the melting point.',src:'aamc-iqb:B20b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Without a reducing agent, a protein elutes from a size-exclusion column earlier than expected for its monomer; with a reducing agent, it elutes at the monomer position. This best indicates:',choices:['intermolecular disulfide bonds','intramolecular disulfide bonds','proteolytic cleavage','partial denaturation'],ans:0,exp:'Eluting earlier means the species is larger, and reversal by a reducing agent shows that **intermolecular disulfide bonds** were covalently linking chains into oligomers. Intramolecular disulfides would not change the number of chains, so they would not make the protein appear much larger.',src:'aamc-iqb:B21'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'A disulfide bond forms between two cysteine thiol groups through:',choices:['reduction','hydrolysis','oxidation','dehydration'],ans:2,exp:'Joining two –SH groups into –S–S– removes two hydrogen atoms (two electrons and two protons), an **oxidation**; reducing agents such as DTT or glutathione reverse it. Disulfides form mainly in the oxidizing environment of the ER and stabilize secreted proteins.',src:'aamc-iqb:B21b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Transferring which side chain from a protein\'s interior to the aqueous surface would have the most positive (least favorable) ΔG°?',choices:['Asparagine','Aspartate','Glutamine','Phenylalanine'],ans:3,exp:'Exposing a large **hydrophobic** side chain like phenylalanine to water forces water to order around it, which is strongly unfavorable; burying such residues is a major driving force for folding. Polar and charged residues such as asparagine, glutamine and aspartate are comfortable at the surface.',src:'aamc-iqb:B22'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'The hydrophobic effect that drives protein folding results mainly from:',choices:['strong attractions between nonpolar side chains','an increase in the entropy of water','new hydrogen bonds formed in the core','a decrease in the entropy of the chain'],ans:1,exp:'When nonpolar side chains are buried, the ordered water "cages" around them are released, so the **entropy of water increases** and ΔG becomes negative. The chain itself loses entropy on folding, which opposes, rather than drives, the process.',src:'aamc-iqb:B22b'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1B',q:'Spontaneous deamination of cytosine in DNA produces:',choices:['thymine','hypoxanthine','uracil','xanthine'],ans:2,exp:'Replacing cytosine\'s amino group with a carbonyl yields **uracil**, which pairs with adenine and would cause a C→T mutation if not repaired; uracil-DNA glycosylase removes it in **base excision repair**. Deamination of adenine gives hypoxanthine, and of guanine gives xanthine.',src:'aamc-iqb:B23'},
  {s:'Nucleic Acids, Membranes & Lab Techniques',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1B',q:'A major advantage of DNA using thymine rather than uracil is that:',choices:['uracil from deaminated cytosine is recognized as damage','thymine forms three hydrogen bonds with adenine','uracil is unable to base-pair with adenine','thymine is cheaper to synthesize than uracil'],ans:0,exp:'Because DNA normally contains **thymine (5-methyluracil)**, any uracil found in DNA can be recognized as damage from cytosine deamination and repaired. Thymine pairs with adenine through two hydrogen bonds, just as uracil does, and it is costlier to make because it is synthesized from dUMP.',src:'aamc-iqb:B23b'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'How many electrons are required to reduce three disulfide bonds to six free thiol groups?',choices:['3','12','6','2'],ans:2,exp:'Each disulfide requires **2 electrons** (plus 2 H⁺) to become two –SH groups, so three disulfides need 6 electrons. Since NADH donates 2 electrons as a hydride, this equals 3 NADH-equivalents.',src:'aamc-iqb:B24'},
  {s:'Metabolism & Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1D',q:'Which molecule is the most abundant cellular thiol reductant that keeps cytosolic protein cysteines reduced?',choices:['Ubiquinone','FAD','NAD⁺','Glutathione'],ans:3,exp:'Reduced **glutathione (GSH)**, a cysteine-containing tripeptide, reduces disulfides and is itself oxidized to GSSG; glutathione reductase then regenerates GSH using NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway. NAD⁺, FAD and ubiquinone are oxidized carriers that accept electrons.',src:'aamc-iqb:B24b'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Cyanogen bromide cleaves a polypeptide on the carboxyl side of which residue?',choices:['Cysteine','Methionine','Lysine','Phenylalanine'],ans:1,exp:'The **sulfur atom of methionine** acts as a nucleophile that attacks cyanogen bromide, leading to cleavage after methionine and conversion of that residue to homoserine lactone. Because methionine is rare, CNBr gives a few large fragments useful in sequencing.',src:'aamc-iqb:B25'},
  {s:'Proteins & Enzymes',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'1A',q:'Trypsin cleaves peptide bonds on the carboxyl side of:',choices:['phenylalanine, tyrosine and tryptophan','methionine and cysteine','aspartate and glutamate','lysine and arginine'],ans:3,exp:'**Trypsin** cuts after the positively charged residues lysine and arginine (unless followed by proline), because its S1 pocket has an aspartate. **Chymotrypsin** cuts after large aromatic residues, and cyanogen bromide after methionine.',src:'aamc-iqb:B25b'},
];
